e('TLS is the most widely deployed cryptographic protocol in the world, and it contains almost nothing new.','TLS is widely deployed to protect network connections.')
e('A secure channel between strangers has to do four things:','This example uses certificate authentication and X25519. TLS 1.3 also supports other groups and PSK authentication; PSK-only mode does not provide forward secrecy. The example has four jobs:')
e('Every full handshake uses ephemeral (EC)DH, so every session has **forward secrecy**:','Certificate-authenticated handshakes use ephemeral (EC)DH, providing **forward secrecy** when ephemeral secrets are securely erased:')
e('Browsers therefore now commonly offer **X25519MLKEM768**:','Chrome introduced **X25519MLKEM768** in version 131:')
e('Both shared secrets are concatenated and fed into the key schedule, so an attacker must break both. Chrome turned it on by default in 2024.','Both shared secrets feed the key schedule. With the specified combiner and correct implementations, either component remaining secure protects the combined exchange. Chrome announced the transition from its earlier Kyber draft to ML-KEM in September 2024.')
e('make sure a TLS 1.3 signature can never be mistaken for a valid signature in TLS 1.2, or in some other protocol using the same key','separate this signature input from other TLS roles and versions, reducing cross-protocol confusion; arbitrary external protocols must still avoid overlapping signed formats')
e('**Every key is different.** Client and server, handshake and application, each have their own secret, so a bug that leaks one does not leak the others, and a message encrypted in one direction can never be reflected back as valid in the other.','**Keys are separated by purpose and direction.** Distinct labels derive client/server and handshake/application traffic secrets. A record key leak need not reveal sibling keys, but a parent secret can reveal its derived keys and a current application traffic secret can derive its future updates. Separation prevents simple reflection between directions under the cryptographic assumptions.')
e('**Every key depends on the transcript.** If an attacker altered any handshake message, the two sides compute different transcript hashes and therefore different keys. The connection fails.','**Traffic secrets bind selected transcript prefixes.** The Early, Handshake and Master Secret extraction steps are not themselves all transcript-dependent. Derived traffic secrets use the appropriate handshake prefix, while signatures and Finished cover later messages. Tampering with a protected transcript is detected.')
