e('TLS 1.2 and earlier also permitted CBC mode with a separate HMAC, applied in the order MAC-then-encrypt. CBC produced a decade of attacks: BEAST exploited predictable IVs, while POODLE and Lucky 13 were padding-oracle and timing attacks made possible by MAC-then-encrypt.','Older TLS suites permitted CBC with a separate HMAC, traditionally MAC-then-encrypt; RFC 7366 later defined an encrypt-then-MAC extension. BEAST targeted predictable IVs in TLS 1.0, Lucky 13 exploited timing in CBC processing, and the original POODLE targeted SSL 3.0 padding.')
e('The **resumption secret** lets the server issue a ticket holding a pre-shared key (PSK) for the next connection.','The **resumption master secret** derives a PSK for each ticket. The ticket is an opaque identity: implementations may encrypt resumption state into it or use it to look up server-side state. NewSessionTicket is sent encrypted; a subsequent ordinary ClientHello exposes the ticket identity unless protected by ECH.')
e('or the server\'s **ticket encryption key**,','or a **ticket encryption key** that recovers sufficient resumption state,')
e('Ticket keys are a quiet weak point. Many servers kept the same ticket key for weeks across a fleet, and stealing it undoes forward secrecy for every resumed session it covers. Rotate ticket keys often (hours, not weeks) and keep them out of persistent storage.','For stateless tickets, compromise of a key that exposes PSKs breaks recorded PSK-only resumed sessions. Fresh DH in `psk_dhe_ke` protects its 1-RTT traffic, but 0-RTT traffic still depends only on the PSK. Limit ticket lifetime and key exposure according to the implementation and threat model.\n\n> [!note] Content gap\n> Fleet-wide ticket-key reuse measurements and a universal hourly rotation rule are omitted: no reproducible deployment evidence or universally applicable policy was available for this review.')
