f(6,'text','Domain separation: distinguish this TLS version and role from other signing contexts and reduce cross-protocol signature confusion',3)
f(6,'workedExample','Different signing contexts must use distinct input formats. TLS 1.3 prefixes the transcript hash with 64 spaces and a role-specific context string. The fixed prefix resists confusion with earlier TLS formats, and distinct client/server labels prevent simple role substitution. This is not a guarantee about an arbitrary external protocol that deliberately accepts identical signed bytes.')
f(7,'prompt','An attacker modifies the ClientHello in a certificate-authenticated TLS 1.3 handshake without ECH. Which mechanisms bind the differing transcripts? Select all that apply; failure at an earlier check can prevent later checks from being reached.')
f(7,'workedExample','The client records the ClientHello it sent; the server records the modified one. The resulting transcript difference affects handshake traffic secrets, CertificateVerify inputs and Finished inputs. In practice, mismatched handshake keys usually cause record authentication failure before CertificateVerify or Finished can be checked. These are overlapping protections, not three sequential failures that must all occur.')
f(8,'explanation','Correct. TLS 1.3 has no RSA key-transport decryption operation. A legacy service sharing that RSA key could still expose a separate oracle, so protocol removal does not fix every deployment using the key.',1)
f(8,'workedExample','RSA key transport decrypts client-supplied PKCS#1 v1.5 ciphertexts. Distinguishable padding behavior can supply an oracle for adaptive decryption or related RSA operations. ROBOT found this old problem in deployed implementations. TLS 1.3 removed RSA key transport and uses RSA only for signatures within that protocol.')
f(9,'prompt','A hypothetical CDN uses stateless tickets whose encrypted state reveals the resumption PSK, shares one ticket encryption key for a month, and resumes with `psk_ke` without ECH. An attacker records complete resumed handshakes and traffic, then steals that key. What is exposed?')
f(9,'workedExample','NewSessionTicket is encrypted on issuance, but the opaque ticket identity is sent in the resumed ClientHello here. Under the specified stateless format, the stolen ticket key recovers the PSK. With no fresh DH input, the recorded transcript and PSK derive resumed session keys. Prefer fresh DH for 1-RTT forward secrecy and constrain ticket lifetime/key exposure. A stateful lookup ticket would require a different compromise analysis.')
f(10,'prompt','TLS 1.3 guidance limits AES-GCM to about 2^24.5 full-size (16 KiB) records per key. A video service streams about 50 MB/s (decimal) over a long-lived connection. Roughly how often must its sending direction rekey, and how?')
f(10,'workedExample','2^24.5 × 16,384 bytes ≈ 3.887 × 10^11 bytes. At 50 × 10^6 bytes/s this is about 7,775 seconds, or 2.16 hours. Send KeyUpdate before the usage bound; it advances this direction\'s traffic secret with the traffic upd label, derives a new key/IV and resets its sequence number. The other direction updates separately. Check the library\'s actual key-usage enforcement rather than assuming automatic rekeying.')
f(11,'explanation','Correct under the hybrid construction\'s assumptions and correct implementation: either component retaining security protects the combined key agreement. Endpoint compromise remains outside that guarantee.',0)
